<h3 id="cc1">1. One canonical form; surface text is a view</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Intentional Programming, Simonyi, 1995 [C1]</td><td>A program is a tree of nodes, each an instance of an intention; users edit the tree and view it in any notation; computational intent can be kept separate from implementation</td><td>A research system at Microsoft; the report describes no determinism or replay model (a transaction file feeds version control, but no replay semantics are given)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unison [C2]</td><td>Each definition is identified by a hash of its syntax tree; names are separately stored metadata</td><td>The hash is of structure: two definitions with the same behaviour and different structure have different hashes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Equality saturation and egg [C3; C4]</td><td>An e-graph holds many equivalent versions of a program at once; a cost model picks one (Tate et al.: a pseudo-Boolean solver over a static cost model; egg: an extractor with a user-provided cost function)</td><td>An optimization and synthesis technique applied per function (intraprocedural in Tate et al.) or over terms in a user-defined language (egg, which has no built-in support for binding); saturation is bounded when it does not terminate, and the e-graph is not kept as the program's stored form</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tacit, 2026 [C5]</td><td>The AST is the source of truth; every valid AST has one byte-exact canonical text; definitions are addressed by BLAKE3 hash; malformed code becomes typed Hole nodes</td><td>One target (LLVM); no state-machine semantics stated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>X07, 2026 [C6]</td><td>Canonical source is a JSON AST, edited with JSON Patch; a 646-code diagnostic catalogue with enforced quickfix coverage</td><td>Canonical bytes, not canonical behaviour</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Codifide, 2026 [C7]</td><td>The canonical form is a typed hypergraph with a deterministic byte form and SHA-256 hash; surface syntax is a projection; intent is part of a definition's identity</td><td>Described by its authors as a contract-and-dispatch language, not general computation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>NAIL [C8]</td><td><code>nail canonicalize</code> normalizes any program; <code>nail check --strict</code> rejects non-canonical input</td><td>An RFC 8785-inspired JSON subset that, in its own words, does not claim full RFC 8785 compliance; byte canonicalization only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>EqNet, Allamanis et al., 2017 [C9]</td><td>Neural representations trained on known equivalence classes so that equivalent expressions get identical or highly similar vectors</td><td>Boolean and polynomial expressions (supervised), not programs</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="cc2">2. A program is a deterministic state machine over a log</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Abstract State Machines, Gurevich, 2000 [C10]</td><td>The paper's title states the result: sequential abstract state machines capture sequential algorithms</td><td>A theorem, not a system: every sequential algorithm, defined by three postulates (sequential time, abstract state, bounded exploration; Def. 6.1), has an equivalent sequential abstract state machine that matches it step for step (Main Theorem 6.13). That the postulates cover all sequential algorithms is argued, not proved</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Event sourcing, Fowler, 2005 [C11]</td><td>"Capture all changes to an application state as a sequence of events"</td><td>A pattern, published as a draft; replay stays consistent only if gateways record external query responses and suppress external updates during replay</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The Elm Architecture [C12]</td><td>Model, View, Update: state, a view of the state, and an update from messages</td><td>One language and one target (the browser)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dedalus and the CALM theorem, Hellerstein, Alvaro et al. [C13; C14]</td><td>Dedalus: Datalog with explicit time, modelling mutable state and asynchronous messaging. CALM: a program has a consistent, coordination-free distributed implementation if and only if it is monotonic (proved by Ameloot et al.; [C14] gives a proof sketch)</td><td>Dedalus and Bloom are research languages and CALM is a theorem; neither emits to other languages</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Durable execution: Temporal, Restate [C15; C16]</td><td>Temporal: workflow code "must be deterministic to support replay". Restate: every step and its result are recorded in a journal</td><td>Replay serves resilience, not program identity</td></tr>
    <tr><td>TigerBeetle deterministic simulation testing, 2026 [C17]</td><td>Logical determinism (like FoundationDB) plus physical determinism: replicas converge to byte-by-byte identical state; real code runs in a simulator</td><td>One database, built by hand to this discipline</td></tr>
    <tr><td>FoundationDB simulation testing [C18]</td><td>"A deterministic simulation of an entire FoundationDB cluster within a single-threaded process"; determinism "allows perfect repeatability of a simulated run"</td><td>A testing discipline for one database, not a program form</td></tr>
    <tr><td>musil [C19]</td><td>An explicit-state model checker as a Python library; its simulate mode runs the real node code on a seeded, replayable simulated network</td><td>Bounded state spaces; it checks the model, and simulation finds bugs rather than proving their absence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AILANG; agent-scroll [C20; C21]</td><td>AILANG: every construct has deterministic semantics, with replayable execution and structured traces. agent-scroll: canonical, byte-deterministic, hash-chained transcripts with a verifier that catches any single-byte change</td><td>AILANG is functional, not a state-machine form; agent-scroll logs conversations, not programs</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="cc3">3. Every target is a projection: write once, emit many</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>MLIR [C22]</td><td>A reusable compiler infrastructure spanning levels of abstraction, hardware targets and domains</td><td>Starts below the source: it has no parser generation or AST construction (Sec. 7), and its paper names translation validation across an extensible dialect ecosystem as an open problem (Sec. 2)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Model Driven Architecture, OMG, 2003 [C23]</td><td>Computation-independent, platform-independent and platform-specific viewpoints</td><td>Petre reports, from Forward and Lethbridge, that keeping the model up to date with the code was the biggest perceived problem (68% agreement) [C24]</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Protocol Buffers; Lumos [C25; C26]</td><td>Define a schema once and generate bindings in many languages (Lumos: Rust and TypeScript for Solana)</td><td>Data shapes, not behaviour; the same Protocol Buffers message can have many binary serializations ("You cannot compare two messages for equality without fully parsing them")</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Forge, 2026 [C27]</td><td>"Many frontends feed one verified intermediate representation, and that representation feeds many backends"</td><td>Board and peripheral descriptions, not general programs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Faber Romanus, 2026 [C28]</td><td>One high-level IR projected to Rust, Go, TypeScript, Swift and Faber, and through a lower IR to LLVM and WASM text, with Metal and CUDA kernels; support is measured and stated per target</td><td>Agreement across targets is measured by corpus coverage, not proved</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Intent, 2026 [C29]</td><td>One source file compiles to Rust, JavaScript and WASM; contracts are enforced at runtime in every target and optionally checked by Z3</td><td>Three targets</td></tr>
    <tr><td>NanoLang [C30]</td><td>Core semantics proved in Coq with no axioms; C is the production target</td><td>PTX and RISC-V are experimental; LLVM and WASM are planned</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="cc4">4. Store intent; derive the implementation</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>FORTRAN, Backus et al., 1957; deductive synthesis, Manna and Waldinger, 1980 [C31; C32]</td><td>Automatic coding from formulas; programs extracted from constructive proofs</td><td>FORTRAN became a compiler; deductive synthesis had no implementation at the time</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Spec-driven development, as classified by Böckeler [C33]</td><td>Three levels: spec-first, spec-anchored and spec-as-source, in which "only the spec is edited by the human, the human never touches the code"; of Kiro, spec-kit and Tessl, only Tessl explores spec-as-source</td><td>The spec is natural language, and Böckeler saw code generated repeatedly from one spec differ; nothing checks that the code is the spec</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Vericoding benchmark, 2025 [C34]</td><td>12,504 formal specifications in Dafny, Verus and Lean; at least one of nine off-the-shelf LLMs, with five attempts each, solved 82% (Dafny), 44% (Verus) and 27% (Lean) of the tasks, and the best single model reached 67.5%, 30.9% and 17.9% (Table 3); adding natural-language descriptions gave no statistically significant gain on the one subset tested (Verina, 157 tasks; Sec. 4.1)</td><td>Specification languages are the proof languages; no runtime form or multi-target emission; among successes about 9% of specs were too weak to capture intent (Sec. 4)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Codifide [C7]</td><td>Every definition carries an intent that compilation never discards</td><td>Contract-and-dispatch scope</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> The vericoding benchmark [C34] is used as an outside benchmark for the step from specification to Form: a slice of its state-machine-shaped Dafny, Verus and Lean specifications is lifted into Forms and the share the RCE gate accepts is reported beside the published vericoding results.</p>

<h3 id="cc5">5. The model proposes; a deterministic gate decides</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>CompCert; seL4 [C35; C36]</td><td>Machine-checked compiler and kernel; CompCert checks an unverified heuristic's output with a verified checker; seL4's proof cost about 20 person-years</td><td>Proof effort per artifact</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sketching, Solar-Lezama et al., 2006 (the counterexample-driven loop later named CEGIS) [C37]</td><td>Against a human-written partial program (sketch) and an executable specification, a synthesizer proposes, a checker returns a counterexample, and the loop repeats</td><td>Proposers are SAT solvers, not learned models; limited to finite programs (bounded input, guaranteed termination), with guarantees only for the bounded instance</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AlphaProof; Clover; AutoVerus [C38; C39; C40]</td><td>A model proposes; Lean, Dafny or Verus checks</td><td>Gate on proofs or code text; no canonical program form</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Agentic model checking, 2026; LLM-driven verified synthesis for concept design, 2026 [C41; C42]</td><td>"Agents propose, solvers verify" (an LLM still triages counterexamples for realism); in the second, invariants alone under-constrained designs, which differed across runs, some of them implausible</td><td>[C41] verifies existing code with LLM-inferred specs; checks are bounded (default unwind 4) and spec-relative, and the authors name over-weak specs that "cause bugs to be missed silently" as the primary failure mode. [C42] synthesizes coordination rules for design models (not code), checked only within a bounded Alloy scope, with an LLM translating the design into Alloy. Both warn that validity is not intent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Vera, 2026; Vow; Thermite [C43; C44; C45]</td><td>Vera: mandatory contracts, proved by the Z3 SMT solver where decidable and otherwise checked at run time: "The model doesn't need to be right, it needs to be checkable". Vow: contracts statically verified by ESBMC bounded model checking. Thermite: contracts with counterexamples on failure and a recorded assurance level (Verus/Z3, Lean, Kani bounded, runtime)</td><td>Gate at the function; no replay or promotion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decision models, a class of concept [C46]</td><td>A model whose entire output is a typed answer (one option from a closed set, a bounded score, or a yes/no probability) with a probability for every option, trained for calibration</td><td>Needs a closed option set. The RCE project builds its own decision head and uses no outside decision model</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="cc6">6. Accepted work is promoted and reused</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Gödel machine, Schmidhuber; Löbian obstacle, Yudkowsky and Herreshoff [C47; C48]</td><td>Self-rewrite only after a proof, under its own axioms, that the rewrite has higher utility than continuing to search; in the straightforward formalism, a proof-based agent cannot trust a successor that uses its own proof system (Löb's theorem), so trust shrinks with each rewrite, and the paper's technical workarounds are only partial</td><td>Theory</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Voyager; STOP; Darwin Gödel Machine; AlphaEvolve [C49; C50; C51; C52]</td><td>A growing library of executable skills; scaffolds that improve themselves (the model's weights are unchanged); archives of self-modified agents (kept if they still compile and can edit code; parents chosen by benchmark score); code evolved under automatic evaluators</td><td>Selection by score or evaluator, not by proof at an interface. Voyager's evaluator is a GPT-4 critic; AlphaEvolve's scores may include LLM-graded feedback, and its correctness came from construction, randomized testing or human and verification review outside the loop</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Getafix; Sorald; TBar [C53; C54; C55]</td><td>Fix patterns mined from past fixes or written as templates, reused on new cases without a model; with perfect fault localization TBar correctly fixes 74 of 395 Defects4J bugs (plausible patches for 101), and 43 with standard fault localization</td><td>Patterns are per bug class, learned or written for one language (Getafix's method itself is language-agnostic); Getafix checks only its top fix, by re-running the static analyzer</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

